::ALG1_U02_L04_GP_Q01::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U02_L04_GP_Q01<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - Extension of MLA.A1.AR.1<br><br>Question:<br>Solve 5x + 3 = 2x + 18. {
~A. x = 7#This does not move variable terms correctly.
=B. x = 5#Correct. Subtract 2x and solve 3x + 3 = 18.
~C. x = 15#This stops after subtracting 3.
~D. No solution#The equation has one value that makes it true.




}

::ALG1_U02_L04_GP_Q02::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U02_L04_GP_Q02<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - Extension of MLA.A1.AR.1<br><br>Question:<br>Solve 4x + 7 = 4x - 2. {
~A. x = -9#The variable terms cancel; do not solve by combining constants as x.
~B. x = 0#When variables cancel, check the remaining statement.
~C. Infinitely many solutions#The remaining statement is false, not true.
=D. No solution#Correct. Subtract 4x to get 7 = -2, a false statement.




}

::ALG1_U02_L04_GP_Q03::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U02_L04_GP_Q03<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - Extension of MLA.A1.AR.1<br><br>Question:<br>Solve 3(x + 2) = 3x + 6. {
=A. Infinitely many solutions#Correct. Both sides simplify to 3x + 6.
~B. x = 0#The equation is true for every value, not only zero.
~C. No solution#The simplified statement is true, not false.
~D. x = 6#The equation is an identity, not a single-solution equation.




}

::ALG1_U02_L04_GP_Q04::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U02_L04_GP_Q04<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - Extension of MLA.A1.AR.1<br><br>Question:<br>Solve 6x - 5 = 2x + 11. {
~A. x = 3#This does not correctly move constants and variable terms.
~B. x = 16#This stops before dividing by 4.
=C. x = 4#Correct. Subtract 2x and add 5 to get 4x = 16.
~D. No solution#The equation has one solution.




}

::ALG1_U02_L04_GP_Q05::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U02_L04_GP_Q05<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - Extension of MLA.A1.AR.1<br><br>Question:<br>What does 8 = 8 mean after solving an equation? {
~A. No solution#No solution occurs when the final statement is false.
=B. Infinitely many solutions#Correct. A true statement means every value satisfies the equation.
~C. One solution#A variable value is not isolated.
~D. x = 8#The variable disappeared, so this is not x = 8.




}

